# tcpdrill, which connections a listener that named a local address takes.
#
# NetX Duo registers a listen against a PORT.  There is no
# nx_tcp_server_socket_listen() that takes an address, so by the time anything
# in bsdsocket.library can object to a SYN the three-way handshake is already
# finished down in the TCP state machine.  Whether `bind(127.0.0.1)` means what
# it says is therefore a decision this library makes on its own, at accept()
# time, and nothing above it can tell a working filter from an absent one --
# both leave a listening socket on the port.
#
# The observation that separates them is the application's: with the filter,
# accept() never hands the connection over, and the peer that opened it is torn
# down.  Without it, the connection is served.  That is the difference between
# `nc -l 127.0.0.1` meaning what it says and quietly serving the whole segment.
#
# THE CONTROLS COME FIRST.  a01 and a02 are the same segment on the same wire
# with a local address that must take it -- the wildcard, and the address this
# interface actually carries.  Without them a refusal is indistinguishable from
# a listener that never worked.
#
# `localaddr` is the only new directive: it sets what `listen` binds to.
# `listen = EADDRNOTAVAIL` and `accept = none` are the negative forms of two
# directives that could previously only succeed.
#
# EVERY CASE TEARS ITS OWN CONNECTION DOWN.  a01 and a02 close, and a close is
# not instantaneous, so without the FIN exchange written out the frame lands in
# the next case's queue and a03's `notx` reads it as a second teardown of a
# connection it never made.  It cost an hour here.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# The wildcard takes everything, which is what it is for.
case a01_wildcard_takes_the_wire
localport 4781
localaddr any
listen
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460
rx A seq=1 ack=1
accept
close
tx FA seq=1 ack=1
rx A seq=1 ack=2

# The address the wire carries takes it too.  10.9.9.1 is the only addressed
# interface, so this is the bind a server on this machine would really make.
case a02_interface_address_takes_the_wire
localport 4782
localaddr iface
listen
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460
rx A seq=1 ack=1
accept
close
tx FA seq=1 ack=1
rx A seq=1 ack=2

# The case the filter exists for.  The handshake completes -- it has to, the
# state machine finished it before this library was consulted -- and then the
# connection is refused: accept() never produces it, and the peer is taken
# down rather than left half-open believing it has a server.
case a03_loopback_bound_listener_refuses_the_wire
localport 4783
localaddr loopback
listen
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460
rx A seq=1 ack=1
accept = none
tx R seq=1
notx 500

# A second SYN on the same port after the refusal.  The listen has to still be
# there: refusing a connection may not cost the listener, or one stray SYN from
# the wire disarms a service for good.
case a04_refusal_leaves_the_listener_armed
localport 4784
localaddr loopback
listen
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460
rx A seq=1 ack=1
accept = none
tx R seq=1
notx 500
peerport 9002
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460

# An address this machine has not got is refused at bind(), before any of the
# above is reached.  10.9.9.77 is on this subnet and belongs to nobody, so the
# refusal is bsd_bind_kind()'s and not a routing accident.
case a05_foreign_address_is_refused_at_bind
localport 4785
localaddr foreign
listen = EADDRNOTAVAIL
